288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (Experimental analysis demonstrates that the A* algorithm outperforms other search techniques in terms of execution time, number of nodes explored, and overall efficiency. The proposed system not only improves the speed and accuracy of organ matching but also provides a scalable and structured decision-making framework that can be extended to real- world healthcare systems. This approach highlights the potential of Artificial Intelligence in solving complex optimization problems in critical domains such as organ transplantation. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The system implements classical search algorithms such as Breadth First Search \(BFS\), Uniform Cost Search \(UCS\), and A* Search. While BFS performs exhaustive exploration and UCS ensures optimal cost-based solutions, the A* algorithm significantly improves performance by combining cost and heuristic evaluation, thereby reducing unnecessary state expansions and avoiding combinatorial explosion. The heuristic used is admissible and consistent, ensuring optimality of the solution. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The problem is further refined by clearly defining actions, transition models, and goal conditions to ensure a systematic exploration of possible matches. A mathematically precise cost function is introduced to evaluate each state based on weighted parameters including transportation distance, time sensitivity, compatibility score, and urgency factor. To enhance efficiency, a heuristic function is designed to estimate the remaining cost and guide the search process towards promising solutions. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (This project presents a Smart Organ Donation Matching System formulated as a structured search problem in the domain of Artificial Intelligence. The primary objective of the system )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (is to efficiently identify optimal donor-recipient matches by considering multiple real-world constraints such as blood group compatibility, organ type matching, urgency level of patients, and geographical distance between donor and recipient. Unlike conventional approaches that rely on manual or rule-based filtering, this system models the matching process using a well- defined state space, where each state represents a potential donor-recipient pair. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
